1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,700
A puddle disappears after a sunny afternoon.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:07,300
Later, rain fills the same hollow again.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:13,200
Water has moved, but much of its journey happened where we could not see it.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:22,000
To understand where rain comes from, we need to follow water through the air, inside a cloud, and back onto the ground.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,700
Start with water at the surface.

6
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:31,000
Energy from the sun helps liquid water change into a gas called water vapor.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,600
This is evaporation.

8
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:41,000
It happens at the surface of oceans, lakes, and rivers, and it can happen in that puddle too.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The water leaves the surface and enters the surrounding air.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,200
The puddle does not need to boil.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,600
Water can evaporate at temperatures well below its boiling point.

12
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:01,000
So the absence of bubbles does not mean the water is staying put.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
A surface can gradually lose water while the change remains invisible to someone standing beside it.

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,500
Plants provide another route into the air.

15
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:15,500
Their roots take up liquid water from the soil.

16
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:21,000
Water moves through the plant, and some is released from the leaves as vapor.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,500
This process is called transpiration.

18
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:29,000
A patch of growing vegetation is therefore part of the movement of water, not just a background to it.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,800
We can make that normally hidden movement easier to notice.

20
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:39,200
Place a clear plastic bag around some leaves, and moisture can collect on its inside.

21
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,800
The water released by the leaves has changed back into droplets.

22
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:49,000
What was difficult to see in the open air becomes visible on the bag.

23
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Once water is in the atmosphere, moving air can carry it to other places.

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
The water that eventually falls over land does not have to come from the ground immediately beneath it.

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,500
Winds transport moisture,

26
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:12,000
linking the surface where water entered the air with places where it may later return.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
To form a cloud, moist air often rises and cools.

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Higher in the atmosphere, the surrounding pressure is lower.

29
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,500
A rising portion of air expands, and that expansion cools it.

30
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:33,000
This is an important step: a change in the air's temperature can change what happens to the water vapor within it.

31
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,500
As the air cools enough, water vapor can become liquid droplets.

32
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:42,000
The change from gas to liquid is condensation.

33
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,500
You can see another example on the outside of a cold glass.

34
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:54,000
Water from the surrounding air collects on the cool surface, forming small drops that may join and run down its sides.

35
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
In the atmosphere, tiny particles of dust, salt, or other material help droplets form.

36
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Water gathers around them.

37
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,500
A cloud contains these small droplets, or ice crystals, rather than simply being a patch of visible water vapor.

38
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The cloud we can see is evidence that water has changed its physical form.

39
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Cloud formation is not a permanent finish line.

40
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,500
Droplets can also evaporate again when conditions change.

41
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:29,000
A cloud's outline can grow, shrink, or disappear as water changes between vapor and droplets.

42
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The visible shape tells us something about conditions in the air, rather than marking a fixed container full of water.

43
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,500
Having a cloud does not automatically give us rain on the ground.

44
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:47,500
Its tiny droplets or ice crystals must grow large enough to fall as precipitation.

45
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:52,500
That word includes rain, snow, and other forms of water falling from clouds.

46
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:57,000
The next part of the story is therefore about growth inside the cloud.

47
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,500
One route begins when droplets of different sizes collide.

48
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:04,200
If they join, a larger drop forms.

49
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,000
More collisions can make it larger still.

50
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
This process is called coalescence, which simply means joining together.

51
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
A raindrop develops through changes inside the cloud; it is not a tiny cloud falling out of the sky.

52
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
There is another route in colder clouds that contain both liquid droplets and ice crystals.

53
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,400
Water vapor can add directly to the ice, allowing the crystals to grow.

54
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,700
Rain formation is therefore not limited to liquid droplets joining.

55
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:39,000
The conditions within a cloud determine which growth processes can take place.

56
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,100
The movement of air matters too.

57
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:46,500
Mountains can force air upward as it meets rising terrain.

58
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:50,600
A weather front can also lift warmer air over colder air.

59
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:55,000
These provide ways to begin the upward movement that helps cloud formation.

60
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
We need to consider what the air is doing, as well as how much moisture it contains.

61
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,300
Thunderstorms need a particular combination: moisture, unstable air, and a way to lift that air.

62
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:11,600
A supply of water vapor alone is not the whole explanation.

63
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:16,000
This is why understanding a storm means looking at several conditions together,

64
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:22,000
rather than assuming that one warm or humid afternoon guarantees a thunderstorm.

65
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,500
Now follow the rain after it reaches the ground.

66
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:31,500
Some water moves across the surface, flowing downhill under gravity.

67
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:34,800
Small flows can join channels, streams, and rivers.

68
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:36,800
This is surface runoff.

69
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:44,000
The rain we noticed at one spot may become moving water farther away, connecting different parts of the landscape.

70
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,600
Some water takes a less visible route.

71
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:51,600
It enters openings in the soil and rock, a process called infiltration.

72
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:57,100
Part can remain in shallow soil, while part travels deeper and replenishes groundwater.

73
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:05,000
Rain reaching a field does not all follow one path: some stays near the surface, and some moves below it.

74
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,800
Underground water usually does not occupy a vast open river beneath our feet.

75
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:15,000
Much of it fills spaces between particles and cracks in rock.

76
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,500
Gravity and pressure help move it through those openings.

77
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:26,000
The speed and direction depend on the material, so its journey can differ greatly from water flowing along a visible stream.

78
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Groundwater can eventually emerge into rivers, springs, or the ocean.

79
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,700
Some travels relatively quickly; some remains underground for much longer.

80
00:06:34,700 --> 00:06:40,000
A river's water can therefore include water that took an underground route before arriving.

81
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,000
What we see flowing at the surface may have a history that extends well beyond the latest rainfall.

82
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,600
The condition of the ground affects how these paths divide.

83
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:56,500
Soil that is already wet may accept less additional water.

84
00:06:56,500 --> 00:07:00,000
Different soils also let water enter at different rates.

85
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,500
The amount and intensity of rain matter as well.

86
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:09,000
Counting the rain alone does not tell us how much water will enter the ground.

87
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:15,700
Roads, roofs, and other surfaces that water cannot easily pass through change the route again.

88
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:21,300
More rain can move quickly into drains and streams instead of soaking into the ground.

89
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:26,200
Building across a landscape can therefore change how water moves after a storm,

90
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:30,000
even though the process began with rain falling from clouds.

91
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
These different paths belong to the water cycle.

92
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:40,200
Water is stored in the atmosphere, at the surface, and below ground.

93
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:44,700
It moves between those places and changes between liquid, gas, and ice.

94
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:52,000
The cycle is not a rule that every drop must visit every stage in exactly the same order.

95
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:58,000
It also explains why water availability depends on more than whether it has rained.

96
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Where water is stored, when it moves, and how clean it is all matter.

97
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,700
Water arriving at the surface still needs to reach a useful place at a useful time.

98
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:13,000
Understanding its route helps us understand the supply we actually depend on.

99
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:15,650
Return to the puddle.

100
00:08:15,650 --> 00:08:19,250
Its disappearance was one visible part of a much larger process.

101
00:08:19,250 --> 00:08:24,150
Rain brought water back to the surface after movement and changes in the atmosphere.

102
00:08:24,150 --> 00:08:31,000
Some of that water will flow away, some will enter the soil, and some will return to the air.

103
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
An ordinary rainy day lets us watch part of that continuing movement.
